WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Using the Data Visualizer tool in App Lab, we can also make scatter plots.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.984
Scatter plots are useful for answering questions about relationships between variables or

00:00:13.984 --> 00:00:17.924
attributes in your dataset. First, we'll want to choose Scatter Plot from the

00:00:17.924 --> 00:00:19.968
dropdown.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:23.968
And let's start with some variables that we've looked at before.

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:27.968
And the x values will look at the total percent of female test takers.

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:29.953
Now, if I wanted to know

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:35.833
about the relationship between the percentage of female CS test takers in a state and the

00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:39.673
percentage of schools in that state that offer an AP program,

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:41.937
I could make this scatterplot.

00:00:43.297 --> 00:00:48.537
Now, this scatterplot shows along the x-axis the total percent of female test takers

00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:49.897
and on the y-axis the

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:56.162
percentage of schools in a state that offer an AP program. Each dot represents a particular state.

00:00:56.802 --> 00:00:59.762
So, for example, we can see that this state right here,

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:06.847
38% of the schools in that state offer an AP program, and 29% of the test takers in that

00:01:06.847 --> 00:01:08.947
state identified as female.

00:01:08.947 --> 00:01:15.891
By looking at this entire graph, we can see what, if any, relationship exists between these two variables.

00:01:15.891 --> 00:01:19.876
We can also use the filter tool at the bottom to

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:21.876
narrow down our investigation.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:28.916
Let's say we are interested in looking at only states where a large amount of tests were taken.

00:01:28.916 --> 00:01:29.860
Let's say 2,000.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.860
We can choose the appropriate value here, Total APCS Exams.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.845
We can choose greater than or equal to.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:44.365
And then we'll find the first value that's larger than 2,000 here:

00:01:44.365 --> 00:01:46.365
2,215.

00:01:47.365 --> 00:01:49.829
Now this graph still shows the same variables.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:56.469
It's just that we're looking at only the states where there were 2,000 or more CS tests taken.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:59.814
The last type of visual we can make is a crosstab.

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:07.314
Crosstabs are useful for looking at datasets that involve some values that are strings instead of numbers.

00:02:08.054 --> 00:02:09.799
So for this type of visualization,

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:11.799
let's look at another dataset.

00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:19.783
This dataset comes from the publication The Root and contains

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:26.383
information about the 100 most influential African Americans. After we import it, we

00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:27.503
can look at it here.

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:39.752
Let's say we wanted to get a sense of the most common type of people included in this list, maybe with respect to

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:42.092
the sector that they're in and their age.

00:02:43.372 --> 00:02:48.372
We can choose those two variables here for our x and y values.

00:02:49.172 --> 00:02:55.297
And now you'll see this table that shows up. Each of these values here in different shades of blue

00:02:55.297 --> 00:02:59.721
show the frequency at which these different combinations showed up.

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:04.801
So, for example, we can see here that in this entire dataset, four of the people on this

00:03:04.801 --> 00:03:09.081
list are age 39 and work in the community sector.

00:03:09.081 --> 00:03:14.186
And we can see that that's the most combination out of all the possible combinations in this dataset.

00:03:14.186 --> 00:03:18.866
There are any number of possible questions you could investigate using these visualizations,

00:03:18.866 --> 00:03:24.571
from understanding the variability in your dataset to starting to uncover patterns and relationships in the data.

00:03:25.611 --> 00:03:29.531
These visualizations will help you gain insight into topics that are interesting

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:31.275
to you and are important to you.
